$$y < -3x + 29$$
$$y > x - 1$$

If $a$ is an integer, what is the greatest possible value of $a$?', NULL, $lyceon_json$[]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, '5', ARRAY['5'], E'Substituting $y = 11$ into the first inequality gives $11 < -3a + 29$, so $3a < 18$, meaning $a < 6$. Substituting $y = 11$ into the second inequality gives $11 > a - 1$, so $a < 12$. The binding constraint is $a < 6$. Since $a$ must be an integer strictly less than $6$, the greatest possible integer value is $5$. Including $6$ is incorrect because $3(6) = 18$ makes $a < 6$ false. Using only the second inequality would give the weaker bound $a < 12$, leading to an incorrect answer of $11$.', NULL, NULL, 'draft', 1, 105, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);





$$4x + 7y = 47$$
$$6x - 7y = -17$$

What is the value of $y$?', NULL, $lyceon_json$[]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, '5', ARRAY['5'], E'Adding the two equations eliminates $y$: $(4x + 7y) + (6x - 7y) = 47 + (-17)$, giving $10x = 30$, so $x = 3$. Substituting $x = 3$ into the first equation gives $4(3) + 7y = 47$, so $12 + 7y = 47$, meaning $7y = 35$ and $y = 5$. A common error is to stop after finding $x = 3$ and report that as the answer. Another error is subtracting the equations instead of adding, which does not cleanly eliminate a variable.', NULL, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);












